# SYNOPSIS

```text
size[4] Tread tag[2] fid[4] offset[8] count[4]
size[4] Rread tag[2] count[4] data[count]

size[4] Twrite tag[2] fid[4] offset[8] count[4] data[count]
size[4] Rwrite tag[2] count[4]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`Tread` asks for <count> bytes of the file <fid> refers to, starting <offset> bytes from its start.
<fid> must be open (see open(5)) for reading: `OREAD`, `ORDWR` or `OEXEC`.
`Rread` carries the bytes, and its <count> says how many: it may be fewer than asked for, and is zero at or past the file's end.
The server returns no more than the negotiated msize less 24, `P9_IOHDRSZ`, which is open(5)'s <iounit>, and cuts a larger <count> to that, as vx-9p's client does before it sends one.
These messages are 9P2000's, types 116 to 119, unchanged on the wire.

A read of a directory returns whole directory entries, each laid out as stat(5) describes, one for each file in it, and never part of one.
Its <offset> must be zero, which starts again from the first entry, or where the previous read of the fid ended; any other is refused, since a directory cannot be read from anywhere else.
A read whose <count> is too small for the next entry is refused rather than answered with nothing, which would look like the end.

`Twrite` asks that <count> bytes of <data> be written to the file, starting <offset> bytes from its start.
<fid> must be open for writing, `OWRITE` or `ORDWR`; a directory is never open so.
A <count> larger than msize less 24 is refused, not cut.
`Rwrite`'s <count> is how many bytes the server wrote; vx-9p's client treats more than it sent as a bad reply.

The `posix` extension (see version(5) and share(5)) gives an open file an offset of its own.
A `Tread` or `Twrite` whose <offset> is `P9_OFFSET_CURRENT`, all ones, uses that offset and moves it on by <count>; one at any other offset does as 9P2000's does and leaves it alone.
A write at `P9_OFFSET_CURRENT` to an open file that appends, opened with `OAPPEND` or set so by `Tdesc`, goes to the file's end, found and written in one step since a server answers one request at a time.
Without `posix`, and on a directory, which has no open file, `P9_OFFSET_CURRENT` is refused.
On a connection with `posix`, the musl back end reads and writes so for POSIX's `read` and `write` of a file, and at an explicit offset for `pread` and `pwrite`.

A read or write the server cannot do yet, such as a console's read before a line is typed or a terminal's from ptyd(4), gets no reply until it can; the framework holds the request and serves it again, and every other request on the connection is answered meanwhile.

`Treadref` and `Twriteref` (see dref(5)) do the same with the data in the client's memory, not in the message; `Tmap` (see map(5)) gives a file's bytes as memory.

